# Line endings are normalised to LF in every working tree, on every platform.
#
# Without this, Git for Windows checks out text files with CRLF. Two things in
# this repository then break, and both broke CI #6:
#
#   1. docs/vendor_pose_check.md records the SHA-256 of config/hexapod.json so a
#      published result can be traced to the table that produced it. A hash of a
#      text file's raw bytes is not stable across platforms -- the same file is
#      cae95c5c on Linux and 2887e918 on Windows.
#
#   2. Git decides a file is binary by looking for a NUL byte in the first 8000.
#      NEITHER PDF IN print/ HAS ONE. Git classified both as text and rewrote
#      their line endings on checkout, which corrupts the file and changes its
#      checksum. A Windows clone would have printed a broken calibration target,
#      or none at all.
#
* text=auto eol=lf

# Not a tidiness preference. See (2) above.
*.pdf  binary
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.zip  binary
